From: Medium1992 <Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 19:28:56 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(sniff): add a discordrtc sniffer for Discord voice

A routing rule protocol ["discordrtc"] now matches the voice flows of the
Discord app: calls, streams and screen sharing. Its first datagram to a
voice server is IP discovery, and the RTP of the call follows on the same
pair of ports, so the rule takes the whole call. There is no domain to
override. Discord in a browser speaks WebRTC, which "stun" takes.

The request is a datagram of exactly 74 bytes: type 1, length 70, the
SSRC, then a 64-byte address that is empty in a request, and the port.
The sniffer checks all but the SSRC and the port, which vary, as zapret's
discord_ip_discovery does. It opens like a STUN binding request, but its
length is no multiple of 4 and the SSRC stands where STUN has its cookie,
so it gets a name of its own rather than "stun". Over UDP it is the whole
first datagram, read apart from those that follow.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
